content.text:
Now rewrite the boot block and add helpers.

Current block:

```js
function bootFailed(err) {...}

let game;
let world;
let ui;

try {
  game = new Game();
  world = new World(canvas, game);
  ui = new UI(game, world, audio);
  ...
} catch (err) {
  bootFailed(err);
}

canvas?.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', ...
```

Replace with:

```js
const isGfxError = (err) => /webgl|graphics|context/i.test(err?.message || '');

function bootFailed(err) {
  ...
  const detail = ...
  const hint = boot.mode === 'none' && boot.webglError ? '...' : ...
}
```

Simpler: bootFailed generic message: "The world could not be drawn. {detail}" with hint updated to say "This can happen if graphics acceleration is disabled or the browser is very old, but your save is safe." But the fallback should prevent most.

Add `replaceCanvas()`:

```js
function freshCanvas() {
  const next = document.createElement('canvas');
  next.id = canvas.id || 'scene';
  next.className = canvas.className || '';
  for (const attr of canvas.attributes) { if (!['id','class'].includes(attr.name)) next.setAttribute(attr.name, attr.value); }
  canvas.replaceWith(next);
  canvas = next;
  return next;
}
```

Careful: canvas may not be in DOM? It is.

Now boot:

```js
let game;
let world;
let ui;

function buildWorld(preferLite) {
  if (!preferLite) {
    try {
      const w = new World(canvas, game);
      boot.mode = 'webgl';
      return w;
    } catch (err) {
      boot.webglError = err?.message || String(err);
      record('webgl', err);
    }
  }
  freshCanvas();
  const w = new LiteWorld(canvas, game);
  w.lite = true;
  boot.mode = 'canvas2d';
  return w;
}
```

Careful: `record('webgl', err)` may call console.error; okay, plus boot.errors. But maybe don't call record (would count as error). It's fine.

Actually wait: if `new World` throws mid-constructor after attaching some window listeners, then we replace canvas. The dead World remains referenced nowhere. Its window keydown handlers reference it; pressing WASD will move a ghost world. It also has an animation? No animation loop inside. Minor overhead. Could try to clean: we can't easily. Accept.

Then:

```js
try {
  game = new Game();
  world = buildWorld(false);
  ui = new UI(game, world, audio);
  game.bind(ui, world, audio);
  ui.bind();
  boot.ready = true;
  window.SAMURAI = { game, world, ui, boot, notice, useLiteView };
} catch (err) {
  bootFailed(err);
}
```

Wait: game.bind calls world.rebuild* which LiteWorld implements. Good.

But `new LiteWorld` might fail too if Canvas 2D unavailable; then propagate to catch → bootFailed.

Also: `world = buildWorld(false)` when WebGL succeeds. `boot.mode` set.

Startup toast: UI.bind doesn't show; startGame does. Add in ui.startGame: if (this.world.lite) setTimeout(() => this.toast('Scroll view active — this browser is using the painted 2D world. All gameplay is available.', 'good'), 900);

Now runtime switch:

```js
function useLiteView(reason) {
  if (!game || boot.mode === 'canvas2d') return false;
  try {
    freshCanvas();
    const lite = new LiteWorld(canvas, game);
    lite.lite = true;
    const prev = world;
    world = lite;
    lite.state = game.state;
    boot.mode = 'canvas2d';
    game.world = lite;
    if (ui) {
      ui.attachWorld(lite);
      ui.toast?.('Graphics acceleration was lost. Switched to scroll view — your save and all gameplay are safe.', 'warn');
    }
    try { prev?.renderer?.dispose?.(); } catch {}
    return true;
  } catch (err) {
    record('canvas', err);
    bootFailed(err);
    return false;
  }
}
```

`prev.renderer.dispose()` on a lost context might throw; wrapped. Actually disposing while lost is fine.

Note total elapsed maybe World's requestAnimationFrame? No.

Context lost handling: replace the notice with a fallback timer.

```js
canvas?.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  boot.contextLost = true;
  ...
  const lostAt = performance.now();
  setTimeout(() => {
    if (boot.mode === 'webgl' && (boot.contextLost || world?.contextLost)) {
      useLiteView('context lost');
    }
  }, 2500);
});
```

But after switchToLite, event listeners remain on old canvas; new canvas has none. Fine. `boot.contextLost` should reset false when using lite. Set boot.contextLost = false in useLiteView.

Render step: `if (world.contextLost) return;` LiteWorld has contextLost undefined → falsy, renders. Good.

Render step also calls world.render() with no args; LiteWorld.render() ignores. Good.

Also update `warnedSlow` notice only when frames>30 and no first frame; if lite works fine first frame happens.

Also `window.SAMURAI` should reflect current world so debugging works. Update in useLiteView: `window.SAMURAI.world = lite` if SAMURAI exists.

Now UI.attachWorld.

Also, note `bootFailed` sets mode? Keep.

Now, when boot fails both, the notice hint says enable hardware acceleration; but Canvas also failed — rare. Update message text to be more accurate.

Let's apply.

