Note section 3 uses the same browser instance launched with GPU enabled, so WebGL will be available — not testing no-WebGL mobile. To simulate no WebGL for mobile, I could block WebGL context creation via addInitScript overriding getContext for webgl types. That's reliable: on the mobile context, addInitScript:

```js
const orig = HTMLCanvasElement.prototype.getContext;
HTMLCanvasElement.prototype.getContext = function(type, ...args) {
  if (String(type).startsWith('webgl')) return null;
  return orig.call(this, type, ...args);
};
```

But three calls getContext('webgl2'); returns null → failure. Also the "fresh canvas then 2d" works since we never actually requested a webgl context (we intercepted before). Good.

Add that to context 2.

