The sky shader uses `dir = normalize(vWorld)` where vWorld is the world position, but the sky sphere is moved to the player position. `normalize(vWorld)` is not the direction from the camera unless the sky is centered at the origin! Since `this.sky.position.copy(this.player.position)` (with y=0 typically), at the ridge (0,0,-204), the sky sphere is centered at the player, but `vWorld` includes the offset, so the shader's gradient is computed relative to the world origin, not the camera. That's a bug! At the estate (origin), it's fine. At the ridge (-204 z), the "up" direction in the shader is skewed: `dir.y` is dominated by the offset, making a weird horizon that is pale/green. This explains the pale band at the ridge and the weird sky.

Fix: compute the direction in the shader from the camera position, or use the local position (`position` normalized) instead of world position. Since the sky sphere is centered on the player and not rotated, `normalize(position)` is the correct view direction (the sphere is a dome around the camera). Use `varying vec3 vDir; vDir = normalize(position);` in the vertex shader and `dir = normalize(vDir)` in the fragment. This also fixes the sun/moon placement: the sun mesh positions are world-position-based (sunDir), and the sky's sun glow should point toward the sun. With the sphere centered at the player, local position direction is the same as the world direction from the camera. Good.

But wait, the stars use `Math.cos(a) * r * 520` — positioned around the player too, fine. And `sunMesh` positions are relative to the player. OK.

So fix the sky shader to use local position. This will make the sky gradient correct everywhere, including the new far locations. Nice catch. This likely also affects the "pale band" at the ridge, which was an artifact of the offset.

Also, this may change the overall look at non-origin locations: currently the sky was wrong at all outer locations (e.g., the temple, quarry). At the temple (-110,-82), the sky gradient was skewed. Fixing it improves everything.

